<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchies of Bremen and Verden</h1><p class="st-meta">1648 CE – 1823 CE · capital Stade</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Two secularised prince-bishoprics between Elbe and Weser that passed from Sweden to Hanover, controlling the approaches to Bremen and Hamburg.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/prince-archbishopric-of-bremen">Prince-Archbishopric of Bremen</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-verden">Prince-Bishopric of Verden</a> had been occupied by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-tyrants">the Thirty</a> Years' War. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> in 1648 they were secularised and granted to the Swedish crown as the duchies of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/free-hanseatic-city-of-bremen">Bremen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verden">Verden</a>, with the government seated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stade">Stade</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The duchies kept their estates and Lutheran church while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> used them as a strategic base. The city of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/free-hanseatic-city-of-bremen">Bremen</a> resisted Swedish claims and remained an imperial free city. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> occupied the duchies in 1712 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-northern-war">Great Northern War</a> and sold them to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/electorate-of-hanover">Electorate of Hanover</a> in 1715, a transfer recognised by Sweden in 1719. Farming in the marshlands and moors was the mainstay.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After Napoleonic occupation the lands returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a> in 1813, a kingdom from 1814. In 1823 they were merged into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stade">Stade</a> administrative region, ending the duchies as distinct units; the area passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1866.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchies-of-bremen-and-verden.jpg" alt="Duchies of Bremen and Verden" width="320" height="271"><figcaption>Image: TUBS · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lower_Saxony_Landschaft_der_Herzogt%C3%BCmer_Bremen_und_Verden.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1648 CE – 1823 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Stade</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Christina of Sweden, Charles XI, George I</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Low German, German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Bremen-Verden</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1736&amp;focus=duchies-of-bremen-and-verden" aria-label="Duchies of Bremen and Verden on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4891 336 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchies of Bremen and Verden lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5211 592L5203 595M5211 592L5210 605M5211 592L5381 440M5211 592L5274 535M5211 592L5219 626M5211 592L5219 626"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5203 595h0M5210 605h0M5381 440h0M5274 535h0M5219 626h0M5219 626h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5211 592h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5211 592h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Stade. <a href="/atlas?year=1736&amp;focus=duchies-of-bremen-and-verden">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
